\details{
The Brier score is defined as
\deqn{BS = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n
  \bigl[y_i(1-\hat p_i)^2 + (1-y_i)\hat p_i^2\bigr]}{
  BS = mean(y * (1 - p)^2 + (1 - y) * p^2)}
where \eqn{y_i \in \{0,1\}} and \eqn{\hat p_i} is the predicted
probability.  Lower is better; a perfect model scores 0.

The scaled Brier score (\code{scaled = TRUE}) expresses skill relative
to the climatological baseline \eqn{BS_{\max}}, yielding 1 for a
perfect model and 0 for the no-skill reference.

\code{sides} names the side on which the finite bound lies. The open side is
reported at the boundary of the score's range, which is \eqn{[0, 1]}
for the raw and \eqn{(-\infty, 1]} for the scaled score: \code{"left"}
yields \eqn{[lci, 1]}, \code{"right"} yields \eqn{[0, uci]} (raw) or
\eqn{(-\infty, uci]} (scaled). Two-sided intervals are clamped to the
same range. One-sided intervals require \code{conf.level > 0.5}.

\strong{Normal interval} (\code{method = "normal"})

A normal approximation for the mean of the per-observation Brier
losses, with standard error \eqn{s_{\mathrm{loss}} / \sqrt{n}}.
This approximation assumes independent observations and is intended
for sufficiently large samples.
With \code{scaled = TRUE}, the standard error is divided by
\eqn{BS_{\max}}, treating the estimated baseline as fixed.
The resulting interval does not account for uncertainty in the
baseline; its coverage may therefore differ from the nominal level.
Prefer \code{method = "boot"} for scaled scores.

